hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"15938","Ya'qub narrated to us, he said, my father said, as Ibn Ishaq narrated to me, from Ya'qub ibn Utba, from Muslim ibn Abd Allah ibn Jundub al-Juhani, from Jundub ibn Makith al-Juhani, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ghalib ibn Abd Allah al-Kalbi, Kalb Layth, to Banu Mulawwah at al-Kadid, and commanded him to raid them. So he went out, and I was in his detachment. We proceeded until when we were at Qudayd, we met there al-Harith ibn Malik, who is Ibn al-Barsa al-Laythi, so we seized him. He said: I came only to become Muslim. So Ghalib ibn Abd Allah said: If you came only as a Muslim, being tied a day and a night will not harm you, and if you are upon other than that, we shall secure ourselves from you. He said: So he tied him with a rope, then left over him a black man who was with us, and said: Stay with him until we pass you; if he contends with you, cut off his head. He said: Then we went on until we came to the valley of al-Kadid, and we camped in the evening after Asr. My companions sent me as a scout, so I headed to a hill overlooking the settlement and lay flat on it, and that was at sunset. A man of them came out and looked, and saw me lying flat on the hill. He said to his wife: By Allah, I see darkness on this hill that I did not see earlier today; look, lest the dogs have dragged some of your vessels. She looked and said: No, by Allah, I miss nothing. He said: Hand me my bow and two arrows from my quiver. She handed them to him, and he shot me with an arrow and put it in my side. I pulled it out and laid it down and did not move. Then he shot me with another and put it in the top of my shoulder. I pulled it out and laid it down and did not move. So he said to his wife: By Allah, my two arrows have pierced him, and had he been a beast he would have moved; when morning comes, seek my two arrows and take them, lest the dogs chew them on me. He said: We waited until their livestock returned, and when they had milked and settled and part of the night had passed, we launched the raid upon them, killing whom we killed of them and driving the cattle. We headed back, and the cry of the people went out to their folk seeking aid, and we went out swiftly until we passed al-Harith Ibn al-Barsa and his companion, and we took him with us. The cry of the people reached us, and there came to us what we had no power against, until when there was nothing between us and them but the valley bottom, a flood came between us and them, sent by Allah the Exalted from whence He willed; we had seen neither rain nor circumstance before it. It came with what no one could stand against. We saw them standing looking at us, none of them able to advance, while we drove them swiftly until we brought them up to al-Mashlal, then drove them down past us, and we outstripped the people with what was in our hands."
